text:
US District Judge Dennis Saylor issued a ruling in the court case challenging the Elimination of D/S Rule (Establishing a Fixed Time Period of Admission and an Extension of Stay Procedure for Nonimmigrant Academic Students, Exchange Visitors, and Representatives of Foreign Information Media) on November 16, 2026 that vacates the entire rule. While we expect that US Department of Homeland Security will likely appeal this summary judgement ruling, this is welcome news as it means implementation of all the of the provisions of the new rule, including the adoption of a fixed expiration date on I-94 record and restrictions in F-1 student academic mobility, will remain paused while litigation continues.

On September 14, 2026, the Court issued a preliminary injunction postponing the effective date of the Final Rule pursuant to the Administrative Procedure Act. US Department of Homeland Security has appealed that preliminary injunction to the United States Court of Appeals for the First Circuit, which remains pending. In its preliminary injunction order, the Court found, in substance, that plaintiffs were likely to succeed on their claim that the Final Rule was arbitrary and capricious, in violation of the APA, because:

DHS failed to appropriately weigh the costs and benefits of the Final Rule;

the proffered rationales—fraud and national security—did not support the agency action;

DHS failed to consider less burdensome alternatives;

DHS failed to respond adequately to significant comments.

Both parties subsequently moved for summary judgment. The issues raised on summary judgment by both sides were virtually identical to those raised at the preliminary injunction phase so the Court adopted and incorporated the same analysis and VACATED the final rule pursuant to the Administrative Procedure Act, 5 U.S.C. § 706, on the ground that it is arbitrary, capricious, an abuse of discretion, and otherwise not in accordance with law.

See previous ISSO news posts for more details about the complex provisions of the final rule and details about the legal challenges. As always, please connect with your ISSO advisor should you have additional questions.
